<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.10.1: Calculate an Average Rate of Change from Two Points</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01416</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.10.1: Calculate an Average Rate of Change from Two Points</md:title>
    <md:uuid>f43b985e-783e-4c5c-9ed1-bad35cca48a4</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Warm Up (5 minutes)</title>
<para>The purpose of this warm up is for students to recall how to calculate an average rate of change from two points. Students will use this skill throughout the lesson and return to the context and data table in the last activity of the lesson.</para>
<title>Launch</title>
<para>Ask students to close their books or devices. Tell students that their job is to think of at least one thing they notice and at least one thing they wonder. Display the table for all to see. Give students 1 minute of quiet time to think individually, and then select 3 – 5 students to share something they notice or wonder about the table. Record and display their responses for all to see. If not pointed out, ask students: “Describe how the data are changing as the value of \(t\) is increasing.” (The values of \(p\) are decreasing at a slower rate.) Ask students to reopen their books or devices and complete the activity. Follow with a whole-class discussion.</para>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>Let \(p\) be the function that gives the cost \(p(t)\), in dollars, of producing 1 watt of solar energy \(t\)<emphasis> </emphasis>years after 1977. Here is a table showing the values of \(p\)<emphasis> </emphasis>from 1977 to 1987.</para>
<table class="os-raise-skinnytable">
    <thead>
      <row>
        <entry><emphasis>\(t\)</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>\(p(t)\)</emphasis></entry></row>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <row>
      <entry>
       0
      </entry>
      <entry>
       80
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        1
      </entry>
      <entry>
       60
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        2
      </entry>
      <entry>
        45
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        3
      </entry>
      <entry>
        33.75
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        4
      </entry>
      <entry>
       25.31
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        5
      </entry>
      <entry>
       18.98
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
       6
      </entry>
      <entry>
      14.24
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        7
      </entry>
      <entry>
        10.68
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        8
      </entry>
      <entry>
        8.01
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        9
      </entry>
      <entry>
        6.01
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
